nillin that is especially common in North America, Asia, and Europe. The type of vanilla used varies by location: in North America and Europe for instance, prominent, smoky flavors are more common, while in Ireland, an anise-like flavor is more frequently used. Many people consider vanilla to be the \"default\" flavor of ice cream (see \"plain vanilla\"), although plain ice cream without any flavouring is also available."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-vanilla-ice-cream"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanilla_ice_cream"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Peach Melba","dish/peach-melba"],["Profiterole","dish/profiterole"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Peach Melba","dish/peach-melba"],["Profiterole","dish/profiterole"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Ice cream","ingredient/ice-cream"],["Peach","ingredient/peach"],["Raspberry","ingredient/raspberry"],["Vanilla","ingredient/vanilla"],["Chouquette","ingredient/chouquette"],["Choux pastry","ingredient/choux-pastry"],["Custard","ingredient/custard"],["Ganache","ingredient/ganache"],["Whipped cream","ingredient/whipped-cream"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/f/f3/Vanilla_Ice_Cream_Cone_at_Camp_Manitoulin.jpg","w":500,"h":749,"by":"Steven Depolo","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vanilla_Ice_Cream_Cone_at_Camp_Manitoulin.jpg"}},"dish/slavonian-honey":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/slavonian-honey","n":"Slavonian honey","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Croatian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Slavonian honey is a specific type of honey producued in the Slavonia region of Croatia. As a food product, it is protected by a designation of geographical origin at the level of the European Union."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-slavonian-honey"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slavonian_honey"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Croatian cuisine","cuisine/croatian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Da mi je nešto slatko","ref":"site/damijenestoslatko-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"damijenestoslatko.com","note":"Croatian baking and dessert blog with English versions of its recipes.","url":"https://www.damijenestoslatko.com/en/author/anamarija","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Croatian cuisine","links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"]]}]},"dish/smetana-dairy-product":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/smetana-dairy-product","n":"Smetana (dairy product)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Ukrainian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Smetana is the English-language name for the different types of sour cream traditionally prevalent in Central, Eastern, and Southeastern Europe, and Central Asia. It is a dairy product produced by souring heavy cream. It is similar to crème fraîche, but nowadays mainly sold with 9% to 42% milkfat content depending on the country. Its cooking properties are different from crème fraîche and sour cream sold in the US, which contain 18% butterfat. It is widely used in cooking and baking."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-smetana-dairy-product"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smetana_(dairy_product)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Cream","ingredient/cream"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Savas Kitchen: Ukrainian recipes","ref":"site/savaskitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"savaskitchen.com","note":"Ukrainian-born home cook Nataliia Savas's section of family Ukrainian recipes.","url":"https://savaskitchen.com/category/ukrainian-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Ukrainian cuisine","links":[["Angel wings","d